   "newText": "For similar puzzles, locate closed holes first (e.g. flood-fill from grid boundary to distinguish exterior background from enclosed background), then replace only enclosed background cells with 4.\n\n## Repeating a special-color shape at matching background locations (evidence: task-4798619d)\nA grid may contain a noisy two-color background (demo examples used 0/3 or 0/8; test used 0/9) plus a connected polyomino in a distinct color. Treat the polyomino's occupied cells as a template. Find every translation, in the same orientation (no rotations/reflections observed), where every template cell currently overlays background color 0; color those cells with the polyomino's color. Preserve original template, all other background cells, and dimensions. The example test's color-5 template had relative coordinates {(0,2),(1,1),(2,1),(2,2),(3,0),(3,1),(4,2),(4,3)} (bounding box 5x4), and matching placements were found at origins (row 2,col 1) and (row 12,col 1), using zero-based coordinates. Both were recolored 5 and submission was confirmed correct.\n\nWhen inspecting a template, derive coordinates directly from the colored cells (ignore intervening zeros/background) and preserve the exact shape. Search translations against background-zero cells only; do not require all cells within the template bounding box to be zero, only occupied template positions. Avoid accidentally coloring background zeros outside those positions."
